---
xwayland/window-manager.c — Two changes

1. Initial position: respect WM_NORMAL_HINTS at map time (weston_wm_handle_map_request)

Before: window->map_request was always set from window->pos, which comes from XCB_CREATE_NOTIFY/XCB_CONFIGURE_NOTIFY
events. In XWayland, windows are typically created at (0,0), so this was usually (0,0).

After: If the app set WM_NORMAL_HINTS with USPosition (user-specified position) or PPosition (program-specified
position) flags, the map_request is now taken from size_hints.x/y instead. These flags mean the app explicitly 
requested a position, and size_hints.x/y is the canonical place that position is stored.

weston_wm_window_is_positioned() was already returning true when these flags were set — but the coordinate actually
used was always the (usually zero) window->pos. This change makes the coordinate match the flag.

2. Client-initiated moves: handle X/Y in weston_wm_handle_configure_request

When an app calls XMoveWindow() after it's already mapped, X11 sends a ConfigureRequest with XCB_CONFIG_WINDOW_X
and/or XCB_CONFIG_WINDOW_Y set. The old code silently ignored these bits.

Now two things happen:
- window->pos is updated immediately (but only for non-maximized, non-fullscreen windows — those positions are
  compositor-managed).
- If the window already has a shsurf (is fully mapped), set_toplevel_with_position is called with the new position
  to push the move into the compositor.

---
desktop-shell/shell.c and kiosk-shell/kiosk-shell.c — Same fix in both

set_toplevel_with_position routes through weston_desktop_api_set_xwayland_position, which calls the shell's
desktop_surface_set_xwayland_position. Previously, this only stored the position hint (shsurf->xwayland.pos /
is_set) — a hint that is only consumed at initial map time in the shell's map() function.

Now: If the surface is already mapped, the position is applied immediately by calling 
weston_view_set_position_with_offset (adjusting for surface geometry) followed by weston_view_update_transform.
The transform update fires the compositor's transform_signal, which triggers each shell's transform_handler, which
calls xwayland_surface_api->send_position back into the XWM to update the X11 frame window position and send the
XCB_CONFIGURE_NOTIFY acknowledgement to the X11 client.
